ld be approximately equal (homoscedasticity).\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nANOVA is applicable when comparing means across multiple groups or conditions. It is particularly useful when:\n- The researcher has categorical independent variables (factors) and a continuous dependent variable.\n- The number of groups exceeds two, as t-tests are not suitable for multiple comparisons due to increased risk of Type I error.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis involves the following steps:\n1. Formulate the null hypothesis (H0: all group means are equal) and the alternative hypothesis (H1: at least one group mean is different).\n2. Calculate the F-statistic, which is the ratio of the variance between the groups to the variance within the groups.\n3. Compare the calculated F-statistic to the critical value from the F-distribution table to determine significance.\n4. If significant, conduct post-hoc tests (e.g., Tukey's HSD) to identify which specific groups differ.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\n- ANOVA can handle multiple groups simultaneously, reducing the risk of Type I error compared to multiple t-tests.\n- It provides a comprehensive analysis of variance, allowing for the assessment of interaction effects in factorial designs.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\n- ANOVA is sensitive to violations of its assumptions, particularly normality and homogeneity of variances.\n- It does not indicate which specific groups are different; post-hoc tests are necessary for that information.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\n- MANOVA (Multivariate Analysis of Variance) for multiple dependent variables.\n- ANCOVA (Analysis of Covariance) for adjusting the means based on covariates.\n- Kruskal-Wallis test as a non-parametric alternative when assumptions of ANOVA are violated.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations:**\nResearchers should ensure that the assumptions of ANOVA are met before conducting the analysis. Data transform